# Line-ending policy. WHY: robot-deployed files run on Linux (bash / python3 / g++) and MUST be LF.
# On 2026-07-08 a git branch re-checkout on Windows (core.autocrlf=true, no .gitattributes) rewrote
# the working tree to CRLF; the app then scp'd a CRLF run_follow.sh to the robot and bash died with a
# syntax error (exit 2) on the \r bytes -- before the node ever started. Enforcing eol here makes the
# checkout deterministic on every machine, so a deploy can never ship CRLF again.

# Robot-deployed / Linux-executed sources -> always LF (autocrlf cannot override an explicit attr).
*.sh     text eol=lf
*.py     text eol=lf
*.yaml   text eol=lf
*.yml    text eol=lf
*.cpp    text eol=lf
*.h      text eol=lf
*.md     text eol=lf

# Windows desktop app + launchers -> CRLF (native PowerShell / cmd / WSH tooling).
*.ps1    text eol=crlf
*.bat    text eol=crlf
*.vbs    text eol=crlf

# Model / recording blobs -> never apply text or eol conversion.
*.onnx   binary
*.engine binary
*.pt     binary
*.rrd    binary
*.zip    binary
